WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.039
When you're running agentic systems, the plan you start with often isn't the plan you finish with.

2
00:00:05.599 --> 00:00:10.079
In this video, we'll work through adaptive planning. We'll look at the difference between

3
00:00:10.079 --> 00:00:14.640
static and dynamic planning, the events that trigger a replanning decision,

4
00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:20.479
and how to bound those cycles so the agent stays anchored to its original goal. Let's get into it.

5
00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:27.440
An agent that finishes a task exactly the way it was scripted is running a static plan. Every step

6
00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:34.080
was authored before execution began, so nothing surprises you at runtime. Change one thing,

7
00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:39.680
let the model decide its next step from what it just saw, and you have dynamic planning.

8
00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:44.959
That shift is where adaptability comes from, and it's also where unpredictability comes from.

9
00:00:44.959 --> 00:00:50.319
Replanning is the narrower act inside that, revising the current plan because something

10
00:00:50.319 --> 00:00:55.840
failed or changed. Hold those apart in your head, because most of what makes agentic systems

11
00:00:56.400 --> 00:01:02.479
hard to reason about traces back to which one is in play at a given moment. These distinctions

12
00:01:02.479 --> 00:01:06.239
show up constantly when you're judging whether an agent is behaving correctly.

13
00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:12.239
The key contrast between static and dynamic planning comes down to one question.

14
00:01:12.239 --> 00:01:18.239
When are the steps decided? With static planning, every action is locked in at design time. You know

15
00:01:18.239 --> 00:01:24.239
what the agent will do before it runs. That's powerful for predictability and auditability.

16
00:01:24.239 --> 00:01:29.599
With dynamic planning, the model figures out the next step at inference time based on what

17
00:01:29.599 --> 00:01:36.000
it's seen so far. That's powerful for open-ended, adaptive tasks, but it means the sequence isn't

18
00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:42.559
pre-audited, and it's harder to predict or verify in advance. In practice, most production systems

19
00:01:42.559 --> 00:01:48.639
blend both. They use fixed structure where they can and dynamic decisions only where the task

20
00:01:48.639 --> 00:01:55.279
genuinely requires it. Static and dynamic planning have genuinely different risk profiles,

21
00:01:55.279 --> 00:01:59.760
and that's worth being explicit about. Static plans are predictable, auditable,

22
00:01:59.760 --> 00:02:05.839
and cost-efficient. Every step is individually testable. When something fails, you can localize

23
00:02:05.839 --> 00:02:10.960
it precisely. Dynamic planning gives you adaptability for goals that can't be fully

24
00:02:10.960 --> 00:02:16.399
pre-specified, but because next steps aren't pre-audited, the system requires stronger

25
00:02:16.399 --> 00:02:22.479
monitoring and fallback mechanisms to stay safe. The exam language here is important. Dynamic

26
00:02:22.479 --> 00:02:28.880
planning increases unpredictability, and that's a known trade-off you're accepting. The engineering

27
00:02:28.880 --> 00:02:35.679
response is to compensate with monitoring, logging, and explicit fallback paths rather than hoping the

28
00:02:35.679 --> 00:02:42.399
model stays on track. Four categories of events force an agent to reconsider the plan it is

29
00:02:42.399 --> 00:02:48.160
currently running. A tool call failure is the most obvious one, where the tool is unavailable

30
00:02:48.160 --> 00:02:54.399
or returns an error, a timeout, or an empty result. Unexpected output is subtler because the tool

31
00:02:54.399 --> 00:02:59.440
worked, but what came back contradicts the assumption the next step was built on. State

32
00:02:59.440 --> 00:03:04.479
changes are environmental, meaning something outside the agent's control shifted while it

33
00:03:04.479 --> 00:03:10.000
was running and made earlier steps invalid. Constraint violations are perhaps the most

34
00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:15.600
critical because a proposed action would cross a goal boundary or safety limit the agent is

35
00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:21.919
supposed to respect. Recognizing which category you are in is the first step to handling it correctly.

36
00:03:23.199 --> 00:03:28.240
Not every surprise requires throwing out the current plan. The decision to replan versus

37
00:03:28.240 --> 00:03:34.399
continue is one of the most important judgment calls in agentic design. Replanning is warranted

38
00:03:34.399 --> 00:03:39.279
when a core assumption the plan was built on has been invalidated. It's not warranted when

39
00:03:39.279 --> 00:03:45.119
the deviation is minor and the original path is still recoverable. The right question to ask is

40
00:03:45.119 --> 00:03:51.119
whether the original goal is still achievable via the current plan with adjustments. Unnecessary

41
00:03:51.119 --> 00:03:57.520
replanning wastes tokens, adds latency, and increases the chance that the agent wanders off

42
00:03:57.520 --> 00:04:01.759
in a new direction that only partially satisfies the original requirements.

43
00:04:02.880 --> 00:04:09.119
Once you've decided to replan, you need to bound the loop. A maximum iteration limit is a hard cap

44
00:04:09.119 --> 00:04:15.119
on how many cycles the agent gets before it escalates or stops. Without it, an agent that

45
00:04:15.119 --> 00:04:21.839
keeps hitting the same obstacle will loop forever. A goal constraint check validates that each revised

46
00:04:21.839 --> 00:04:28.399
plan still satisfies the original task before execution resumes. That check is what prevents

47
00:04:28.399 --> 00:04:34.160
goal drift, which is the subtle failure where the agent solves the immediate obstacle and quietly

48
00:04:34.160 --> 00:04:40.959
stops serving the actual goal. Loop termination follows from both. Stop at the cap or stop when

49
00:04:40.959 --> 00:04:47.359
the new plan clears every constraint. Preserve the original goal as a persistent artifact and

50
00:04:47.359 --> 00:04:54.959
log each revision against it so drift is detectable after the fact. The failure I worry about here

51
00:04:54.959 --> 00:05:00.799
isn't an agent that crashes. It's one that quietly beats the obstacle in front of it and stops

52
00:05:00.799 --> 00:05:06.239
serving the goal that you actually gave it from the start. Revision after revision, the original

53
00:05:06.239 --> 00:05:14.000
intent fades out of the run. A hard cap on cycles plus a constraint check before execution resumes

54
00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:19.679
is what holds everything in place. That closes out this lesson. I'll see you in the next one.

